<page type="text">
  <layout>One column of running text, with no marginal notes visible. Page number printed at the head. The page continues below the cut, so this part ends mid-paragraph.</layout>
  <page_number>(345)</page_number>
  <main_text>
    <p cont="true">l.2.c.4.) non animæ rëqiri alimenta, sed corpori, curâ quidem illius, sed tanquam in domo habitantis, &amp; moribundæ carnis imminentes ruinas providâ refectione fulcientis. ita rei ipsius rationem reddidit Lucretius, phraseos Vincentius, dum corpus domicilii instar labantis habere suggerit, quod cibo potúque tanquam destinis ac tibicinibus qibusdam suffulciri &amp; sustentari necesse sit. cibo, inquam, potúque. nam &amp; de isto Seneca (licet avdacius forsan pavlo videatur) <i>epist.95. Vino fulcire venas cadentes.</i> quod idem <i>de benefic. l.3.c.9. cadentes venas vino reficere</i>, dixit.</p>
    <p>Quod autem <i>stomachum</i> Latinus reddidit, quod Hebraicum textum secuti Græci γαστέρα dixere, nec illud incommodè prorsus. siquidem Græci veteres τὸ σῶμα τῆς κοιλίας sive τῆς γαστρὸς, <i>i.e. stomachum</i>, γαστέρα dixere: uti pluribus in locis monuit Galenus, <i>de aliment. facult.l.2.c.24. de locis affect.l.5. c.8. comment. in Hippocr. de humoribus l.3. §.30.</i> idémque variis, ex avctoribus primæ notæ, exemplis confirmat. <i>de Hippocr. ac Platon. dogmat.l.2.c.9.</i> inde γαστριαλγία &amp; γαστριαγμὸς, non cordis, sed ventriculi ἀλγήματα τῆ δήξεις, dolores morsúsque: uti idem docet <i>ad aphorism.l 4. aph.65. &amp; in prognost.comment.3. §.36.</i> Idem γαστριαλγεῖν exponit τὸ σῶμα τῆς κοιλίας ὀ-[illegible, ~8 letters], <i>in Epidem.l.1. comment.2. §.[?]</i></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The Greek words are set in a small italic type and several are uncertain; marked [?] where doubtful. The last line is cut off at the lower edge of this part.</notes>
</page>
